<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Habr Yunis Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1787 CE – 1907 CE (approx.) · capital Toon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Somali sultanate of the Habr Yunis clan that broke away from the Isaaq Sultanate and played a part in the early Dervish movement.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habr-yunis">Habr Yunis</a>, one of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/isaaq-sultanate">Isaaq</a> clans, set up their own sultanate around 1787, breaking away from the Isaaq Sultanate after a dispute over succession and authority. Its sultans were based near Toon and later in the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burao">Burao</a> in northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/somalia">Somalia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Like other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> sultanates it was a clan polity, with the sultan presiding over elders and mediating conflicts according to customary and Islamic law. Its people herded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and traded through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Nur Ahmed Aman was among the leaders who opposed British and missionary influence and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-abdullah-hassan">Mohammed Abdullah Hassan</a> in founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dervish-state">Dervish movement</a> in the 1890s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habr-yunis">Habr Yunis</a> signed protection treaties with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> in the 1880s and became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-somaliland">British Somaliland</a>. The sultanate's political role faded in the early 20th century, though the title survives as a traditional office.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/habr-yunis-sultanate.jpg" alt="Habr Yunis Sultanate" width="320" height="174"><figcaption>Image: Stieler, Adolf · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Waram.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1787 CE – 1907 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Toon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nur Ahmed Aman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Somali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/somalia">Somalia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Habar Yoonis Sultanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1847&amp;focus=habr-yunis-sultanate" aria-label="Habr Yunis Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5934 2044 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Habr Yunis Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6216 2076h0M6240 2044h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6254 2300h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6254 2300h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Somalia. <a href="/atlas?year=1847&amp;focus=habr-yunis-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
